WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.920
OK, next question today, David. Can Democrats&nbsp;
add Puerto Rico in Washington, D.C., a states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:12.320
without Republican involvement? So there's&nbsp;
two answers to this. Legally speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.440
constitutionally speaking, admitting a&nbsp;
new state is not a legislative matter.&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:25.200
So the legislative filibuster that is possible&nbsp;
in the Senate on some issues should not actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:33.600
apply. And as the week recently published in&nbsp;
recent years, both Democrats and Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.680
have in effect agreed the filibuster shouldn't&nbsp;
apply to certain constitutional matters not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.840
covered by Article one, which lays out the&nbsp;
design of Congress and its legislative powers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:52.960
The admission of new states is not part of Article&nbsp;
one, and it's something distinct. And as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:01.520
in theory, Democrats don't need Republicans to&nbsp;
make Washington, D.C. or Puerto Rico states. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:12.240
historically, this is a different matter.&nbsp;
The idea from the framers appears to be they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:20.560
clearly designated the addition of states as not&nbsp;
requiring a super majority. But at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:26.880
it is not particularly likely that&nbsp;
from a practical political standpoint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.520
Democrats would go it completely alone. And&nbsp;
from the people I've spoken to that are aides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.560
to elected officials, there is the belief that&nbsp;
it is, if it is not bipartisan to some degree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:47.600
that it would be unlikely to happen. And so,&nbsp;
although from a very fundamental legal standpoint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:54.080
Democrats could say we are voting to make&nbsp;
D.C. a state and it could be 50/50 in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.000
Senate and then Kamala Harris could cast the&nbsp;
deciding vote. That certainly could be done.&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:02.240
I have not spoken to anyone who thinks that&nbsp;
it's going to actually be done that way and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.040
that more than likely, if Democrats were dead&nbsp;
set on making D.C. and or Puerto Rico states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:11.840
even if Republicans weren't thrilled with it,&nbsp;
that there would be some kind of negotiation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:15.600
where Republicans would get something. Now,&nbsp;
what that something is, I don't actually know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.160
One other thought on this. I don't know&nbsp;
for sure that every Democrat in the Senate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.760
in the absence of some Republicans who want to&nbsp;
make D.C. and or Puerto Rico states, I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.760
know that every Democratic senator would actually&nbsp;
go for it. So there's actually a question as to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:37.360
whether you get to 50/50 with Joe Manchin vote&nbsp;
to make Puerto Rico and D.C. and or DC states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:40.640
if not a single Republican in the Senate&nbsp;
plans to do it. I don't know the answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:46.240
to that. And there is the feeling that there&nbsp;
are at least one or two Democratic senators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.520
who, without some Republican&nbsp;
support for the additional states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.240
would not go for it. The real question to me is,&nbsp;
are we ever going to find out the answer to this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.640
Is this actually going to be something Democrats&nbsp;
try to do in the next two years as they control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.200
the White House, the House and the Senate? I wish&nbsp;
I could say yes. It's not completely clear to me.

